<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
        The one permission this app did not have, and the reason it now does.

        There is no store listing. A build reaches a phone as an APK from the
        releases page, which means the app cannot be updated by the platform
        and a person running a version with a known bug in it has no way of
        finding out. That is what this is for and it is all it is for.

        What it does: one HTTPS GET to the releases API, on opening the app and
        at most once a day, or when the update button in Settings is pressed.
        Nothing is sent — no identifier, no statistics, no card, no answer;
        the request has no body and carries nothing but a version number in
        the user agent. The reply is a few kilobytes of JSON: a tag, a file
        size and the release notes.

        The same socket now also fetches the APK, once the update button is
        pressed and never before: a second HTTPS GET, to the file the release
        listed, straight into the app's own cache with a band and a percentage
        in front of it. Still nothing sent, still nothing read from the phone.

        It can be switched off in Settings, and switched off it opens no socket
        at all.

        The review log still never leaves the phone. See docs/UPDATES.md.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
        Asking to install the file that was just downloaded.

        Until 0.4.0 press this permission was deliberately absent and the link
        was handed to the browser instead. What that argument left out is the
        person on the other end: an app that leaves, a file that lands in a
        folder they then have to find, and a notification that looks like every
        other one they ignore. The update was available and nobody installed it.

        What this permission is: the right to ask. It does not install anything
        and it cannot. The file is handed to the system installer through the
        provider below, and the platform's own prompt — the one that names the
        app, says it is an update and checks the signature — is what decides.
        Android also requires the user to switch this on per app, on a settings
        screen; the app opens that screen at the moment it is needed and never
        on first launch.

        What it is not: a way to install anything else. The only URL fetched is
        the asset the releases API named, the only file written is in the app's
        cache under updates/, and a build signed with a different key is refused
        by the platform, not by this code.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES" />

    <!--
        Android 11 and later hide other installed packages by default, and a
        speech engine is another package. Without this the platform answers
        "no engine" on a phone that has one, and the speaker mark would never
        appear. This asks for visibility of speech services only.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.TTS_SERVICE" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <!--
        allowBackup is off, and that is a privacy decision rather than a
        technical one.

        With it on, Android copies the app's data — which here means the review
        log: every phrase studied, every rating, every timestamp — into the
        user's Google account. PRIVACY.md promises that nothing leaves the phone,
        and the whole point of the export is that the user moves that file
        themselves, on purpose, to a place they chose. Uploading it silently to a
        third party contradicts both.

        Local device-to-device transfer is still allowed, in
        data_extraction_rules.xml: it is a direct copy to the new phone, with no
        server in between.
    -->
    <application
        android:name=".IknaApp"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:localeConfig="@xml/locales_config"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Ikna">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Ikna.Launch">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
            The home screen widget. Not exported: the only sender of
            APPWIDGET_UPDATE that matters is the system, which delivers to the
            component directly, and nothing else has any business poking it.
        -->
        <!--
            How a deck leaves the phone.

            Sharing needs a URI another app can read, and the alternative to a
            provider is world-readable storage, which is worse. This one is not
            exported, grants permission per-intent, and is pointed at two cache
            directories (see file_paths.xml): the deck being shared right now,
            and the update being installed right now. Nothing else is reachable
            through it, and the review log is not under either path.

            The installer is handed a URI from here for the same reason another
            app is: a file:// URI would have to be world-readable, and the grant
            this makes lasts for one intent and one file.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.files"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
        </provider>

        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.TodayWidget"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/widget_today_info" />
        </receiver>
    </application>
</manifest>
